"""Definitions and selection reasoning, deliberately distinct from implementation code."""
DEFINITIONS = {
 'hash': ('A hash map stores key→value associations; a set stores membership. The pattern records past information so later values can be matched or counted without rescanning.', 'Use it when the next decision depends on whether a value appeared, how often it appeared, or where it appeared.', 'Two Sum: reading 7 with target 9 asks whether 2 is already in the map. If 2 was at index 0, return [0,1].'),
 'pointers': ('Two pointers are positions that move through a sequence according to an elimination rule. They may approach each other, move together, or act as read/write positions.', 'Use it when order or a structural property lets you prove which candidates can be discarded after each move.', 'Sorted pair [2,7,11,15], target 9: 2+15 is too large, so move right leftward. Then 2+11 is too large; finally 2+7 matches.'),
 'window': ('A sliding window is a contiguous region [left,right] whose summary is updated when its boundaries move. It reuses work from the previous region instead of recalculating it.', 'Use it when you can maintain and repair a validity condition cheaply as elements enter and leave. Both boundaries typically move only forward.', 'For "abba", add a then b. Adding the second b creates a duplicate; remove a, then the first b. The window becomes "b", then "ba". The best length stays 2.'),
 'prefix': ('A prefix summary records the aggregate of all elements before each boundary. An invertible aggregate lets a range be recovered by combining two boundaries. A difference array instead records where batched updates start and stop.', 'Use prefix sums for many static range queries or exact subarray sums. Prefix/suffix products can combine information from both sides without division.', '[2,-1,3] has sum prefixes [0,2,1,4]. Inclusive range [1,2] is prefix[3]−prefix[1] = 4−2 = 2.'),
 'sort': ('Sorting arranges values under an explicit order. A sweep line then processes ordered events while maintaining a summary of the active items.', 'Use it when ordering turns a global relationship into a local comparison or a timeline of arrivals/departures.', 'Meetings [0,10] and [5,15] become start/end events. Active rooms progress 0→1→2→1→0; the maximum is 2.'),
 'intervals': ('An interval represents every point between two endpoints. Interval patterns merge overlapping ranges, intersect them, or choose compatible ranges under an endpoint convention.', 'Use it when the objects are ranges rather than individual values. Sort by start for merging, or by finish for many greedy scheduling tasks.', 'Closed intervals [1,3] and [2,6] overlap and become [1,6]. [8,10] remains separate. Touching endpoints depend on whether ranges are closed or half-open.'),
 'binary': ('Binary search repeatedly halves a search interval that is known to contain a target or a boundary between false and true outcomes.', 'Use it on sorted values or a monotonic feasibility condition. You must identify what is being searched and why the eliminated half cannot contain the answer.', 'For [1,3,5,7], target 5, mid initially points to 3. Discard indices through 1; the next mid points to 5. Bounds must shrink on every miss.'),
 'fast': ('Fast/slow pointers repeatedly follow one successor at different speeds. Their relative movement exposes a cycle or positions one pointer halfway through a chain.', 'Use it for singly linked structures or implicit functional graphs when you want cycle/midpoint information without a visited set.', 'On a four-node list, after one round slow is at node 2 and fast at node 3. After two rounds slow is at node 3 and fast is past the end: node 3 is the second middle.'),
 'linked': ('Linked-list rewiring changes node references instead of rearranging an array. The central challenge is preserving reachability while links are replaced.', 'Use it when the input consists of nodes and next references. Save any links you will need before overwriting them.', 'For 1→2→3, save next=2 before setting 1.next=None. Then save next=3 before setting 2.next=1. Finally 3.next=2, producing 3→2→1.'),
 'stack': ('A stack processes the most recently unresolved item first. A monotonic stack additionally keeps values increasing or decreasing, removing candidates once a new value resolves or dominates them.', 'Use ordinary stacks for nesting/postfix parsing and monotonic stacks for next greater/smaller relationships.', 'Temperatures [73,74,72]: push index 0. Reading 74 pops index 0 and records distance 1. Index 1 remains unresolved; 72 is pushed after it.'),
 'deque': ('A deque supports efficient removal at both ends. A monotonic deque retains only candidates that can still be the minimum/maximum of a moving window, in arrival order.', 'Use it when candidates both expire by age and become dominated by newer values. Ordinary queues also support FIFO streaming summaries.', 'For [1,3,-1] with k=2, reading 3 removes the dominated 1. The next window retains 3 ahead of −1; its front is still the maximum.'),
 'heap': ('A heap is a partial order exposing the smallest element at its root. A priority queue uses this to repeatedly select a best pending item; a fixed-size heap can retain top-K candidates.', 'Use it when you repeatedly need the next best value, especially as the set changes. Iterating the heap does not produce globally sorted order.', 'To retain the two largest values in [3,1,5], keep a min-heap of size 2: [3], then [1,3], then insert 5 and remove 1. Root 3 is the second largest.'),
 'dfs': ('Depth-first search explores one branch fully before returning to other branches. Tree recursion uses returned child results to calculate a result for the current subtree.', 'Use it for reachability, components, structural tree properties, and computations naturally decomposed into child subtrees.', 'Tree depth: an empty child returns 0, a leaf returns 1, and its parent returns 1+max(left_depth,right_depth). Each call owns its own local state.'),
 'bfs': ('Breadth-first search explores a frontier in layers. In an equal-edge-cost graph, first discovery gives the shortest number of transitions from any starting node.', 'Use it for minimum equal-cost moves, tree levels, and spreading processes. Seed multiple starting nodes when the nearest of several sources matters.', 'From A with neighbors B,C, visit A at distance 0, B/C at distance 1, then their unseen neighbors at distance 2. Mark on enqueue so each node enters the queue once.'),
 'backtrack': ('Backtracking explores a tree of partial candidates by making a choice, recursing, and undoing that choice. Invalid partial candidates can be pruned.', 'Use it to enumerate combinations, arrangements, or constrained paths when the choices cannot be collapsed into a small direct computation.', 'Subsets of [1,2]: choose/skip 1, then choose/skip 2. Store copies at leaves; after exploring a choice, pop it so the next branch begins with the correct path.'),
 'greedy': ('A greedy algorithm makes a locally preferred choice and commits to it. It is correct only when you can show this choice is compatible with a globally optimal solution.', 'Use it when an exchange or dominance argument proves discarded choices cannot improve the answer. The word maximum alone is not enough.', 'For non-overlapping meetings, choose the earliest finishing compatible meeting. It leaves at least as much room for later meetings as any compatible alternative.'),
 'dp': ('Dynamic programming stores solutions to states that recur across different decision paths. A state summarises all history relevant to future choices; a recurrence relates it to smaller states.', 'Use it when subproblems overlap and the state is small enough to compute. Define the exact meaning of each cell before writing loops.', 'Unique Paths at cell (r,c): dp[r][c] = dp[r−1][c] + dp[r][c−1]. A 3×3 matrix becomes [[1,1,1],[1,2,3],[1,3,6]]. The complete trace shows each assignment.'),
 'graph': ('A graph represents entities as vertices and relationships as edges. Different graph methods solve different properties: topological ordering for dependencies, union-find for connectivity, and shortest-path algorithms for costs.', 'Choose from edge direction, weights, cycles, and the requested output. Graph is a model, not one universal algorithm.', 'Prerequisite A→B means A must be processed first. Removing zero-indegree A reduces B’s indegree; if no ready vertex remains while nodes are unfinished, a directed cycle exists.'),
 'trie': ('A trie stores characters along shared prefix paths, marking which paths are complete words. String-matching methods reuse partial matches rather than restarting comparisons.', 'Use a trie for prefix-aware dictionaries. Use KMP for finding a fixed pattern in text; use simple prefix comparison when its constraints suffice.', 'Insert "cat" and "car": both share c→a, then branch to t/r. A word marker at a node distinguishes an exact word from a prefix.'),
 'bits': ('Bit and mathematical patterns exploit algebraic structure, binary representation, cancellation, divisibility, or repeated squaring instead of generic search.', 'Use them only when the input’s mathematical guarantees support the identity being applied.', '[4,1,1] reduces under XOR as 4 XOR 1 XOR 1 = 4, because paired equal values cancel. This rule fails if the repetition-count assumption changes.'),
 'matrix': ('Matrix/simulation patterns make coordinate order, layer boundaries, and update timing explicit. A two-dimensional index is a pair; each transformation must preserve the source data it still needs.', 'Use them for grid traversal and state evolution. Distinguish in-place updates from simultaneous updates using an old/new generation.', 'Rotating [[1,2],[3,4]]: transpose gives [[1,3],[2,4]], then reverse rows to obtain [[3,1],[4,2]]. Each swap changes both cells simultaneously.'),
}

CONSTRAINTS={
 'Two Sum':['nums contains integers; target is an integer.','Return two distinct zero-based indices; the practice contract assumes one valid pair.','Duplicate values are allowed. Look up the complement before inserting the current index.'],
 'Longest Unique Substring':['The selected region must be contiguous.','Characters may repeat; counts refer only to the current window.','Empty text returns 0. Do not confuse substring with subsequence.'],
 'Product of Array Except Self':['Values may include zero, negatives and duplicates.','Do not use division. Prefix/suffix products exclude the current element.','This practice function accepts an empty list and returns an empty list.'],
 'Merge Intervals':['Each closed interval has start ≤ end.','The input need not be sorted; empty input returns an empty result.','Touching closed intervals merge; half-open meeting semantics differ.'],
 'Subarray Sum Equals K':['Values and k are integers; negative values and zero are allowed.','Count nonempty contiguous subarrays, not subsequences.','Use frequency counts of prefixes, including the leading zero prefix.'],
 'Minimum Size Subarray Sum':['Every value and target must be positive.','Return the shortest nonempty contiguous region with sum ≥ target.','Return 0 if no such region exists; negative values need a different approach.'],
 'Lower Bound':['nums is sorted in nondecreasing order; duplicate values are allowed.','Return the first index with value ≥ target, not necessarily an exact match.','Return len(nums) when no such position exists; empty input returns 0.'],
 'Coin Change':['Coin values are positive integers; amount is a nonnegative integer.','Unlimited reuse is allowed; denominations may not form a greedy coin system.','Return −1 if no combination forms amount; amount 0 uses zero coins.'],
 'Unique Paths':['m and n are positive integers.','Only right and down moves are legal; there are no obstacles in this version.','Rows must be distinct list objects so one cell update does not alter another row.'],
 '01 Matrix':['Input is rectangular and binary.','A nonempty input must contain at least one zero.','Moves use four-directional neighbors; distances are measured in steps.'],
}

def teach(patterns):
    for p in patterns:
        p['definition'],p['reasoning'],p['concreteExample']=DEFINITIONS[p['id']]
        for e in p['examples']:
            e['constraints']=CONSTRAINTS.get(e['title'],[e['prompt'],f'Let n describe the input size: the implementation’s stated time cost is {e["time"]}. Check that against the actual problem’s limits.', 'Only test inputs allowed by this stated contract. Numeric bounds from the real assessment are not known.'])
